      "text": "To solve the equation \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\), we start by taking the square root of both sides of the equation:\n\n\\[\n(x - 5)^2 = 9\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get two possible equations:\n\n\\[\nx - 5 = 3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x - 5 = -3\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve each equation for \\(x\\):\n\n1. From \\(x - 5 = 3\\):\n   \\[\n   x - 5 = 3\n   \\]\n   Adding 5 to both sides gives:\n   \\[\n   x = 3 + 5\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = 8\n   \\]\n\n2. From \\(x - 5 = -3\\):\n   \\[\n   x - 5 = -3\n   \\]\n   Adding 5 to both sides gives:\n   \\[\n   x = -3 + 5\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = 2\n   \\]\n\nThus, the roots of the equation are \\(x = 8\\) and \\(x = 2\\). To find the sum of the roots, we add these values together:\n\n\\[\n8 + 2 = 10\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the roots of the equation \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10}\n\\]",
