      "text": "1. Start by expanding the squared term:\n   $$ (x - 5)^2 = 9 $$\n   $$ x^2 - 10x + 25 = 9 $$\n\n2. Recognize that a perfect square can be factored as $(a-b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab - b^2$.\n   $$ x^2 - 10x + 25 = -9 $$ \n\n3. To make it match the form of a difference of squares, we add the square of half the coefficient of x to both sides:\n   (10 is the coefficient of x)\n\n   ```2. To identify half of the coefficient by dividing it by 2, add \n   (5 is half of 10)```\n   So, we add $(-5)^2 = 25$ to both sides:\n   x^2 - 10x + (5^2) = 6^2 - (5^2)\n   x^2 - 10x + 25 = 36 - 25\n   x^2 - 10x + 25 = 11 \n\n4. Next, factor the left-hand side into a perfect square:\n   x^2 - 10x + 25 = (x-5)^2 \n   \n   Hence,\n   (x - 5)^2 = 11 \n\n5. To find the roots, set each factor equal to zero:\n   x-5 = 11 - 5 \n        = 6\n   Similarly,\n   x = 5 + 11 \n        = 16 \n\n6. Therefore, the roots are x = 6 and x = 16.\n\n7. Finally, sum the roots to find the answer:\n   16 + 6 \n   = 22.\n\nAnswer: the sum of the roots is $\\boxed{22}$.",
